coder › Done. Here's what I changed and why.

## What was wrong

`Queue.push` called `job()` immediately:

```ts
this.#pending.push(
  job().catch(...)
)
```

so every job started the moment it was pushed — concurrently with any earlier job still running — and `drain` only awaited the snapshot of `#pending` taken at call time.

## The fix (`src/queue.ts`)

Replaced the parallel `#pending` array with a single promise chain tail, `#tail`:

- `push` does `this.#tail = this.#tail.then(() => job()).catch(...)`, so each job is scheduled on the promise that only settles after the previous job (and its error handling) finished — jobs run strictly in push order, one after another.
- The `.then` callback runs in a microtask, never synchronously inside `push`; `push` stays a synchronous `void` function. The queue is still "hot": the first job is scheduled on an already-resolved promise, so jobs run without anyone calling `drain` (preserving the old behavior of work starting at push).
- Each job's rejection is caught and recorded in `errors` (Error instances kept as-is, anything else wrapped in `Error(String(cause))`, as before), leaving the chain resolved so later jobs still run.
- `drain` loops: it awaits the current tail, then checks whether the tail changed while awaiting (i.e. jobs were pushed during draining); if so it keeps waiting, so it covers jobs pushed while draining and settles when the queue is quiescent.

## Tests (`src/queue.test.ts`)

Added alongside the original test, covering: no synchronous run inside push but the job still runs on its own; strict push-order execution; a job only starts after the previous one finishes (second job held back by a gated first job); thrown errors recorded and later jobs still run (including a thrown non-`Error` value, wrapped as an `Error`); `drain` waiting for jobs pushed while it is draining; and `push` returning nothing.

## Checks

The package's only script is `bun test` (no typecheck/lint). Output:

```
 7 pass
 0 fail
 17 expect() calls
Ran 7 tests across 1 file. [43.00ms]
```

Re-ran 5 times to confirm the timer-based tests are stable — all passed each time.

Commits on `work/main`: `6199a6b` (fix), `4aeb25c` (tests).

One decision the request didn't spell out: jobs still auto-run after being pushed (asynchronously) rather than only running once `drain` is called — I kept that because the original code started jobs at push time and the stated bug is about concurrency, not about jobs needing an explicit kick. The `drain`-while-draining test pins down that behavior.

